          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for an uncomplicated urinary tract infection in a non-pregnant adult?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe guidance describes an uncomplicated urinary tract infection as bladder infection (acute cystitis) in a non-pregnant adult with a normal urinary tract and normal immune function. Source: *Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence* (Infectious diseases and urology society guidance).\n\nAnything outside that definition is handled on a different pathway. The listed exceptions are pregnancy, being a man, catheters, structural problems, kidney stones, weakened immunity, and suspected kidney involvement. The general reference (*Urinary tract infection*) adds that infection reaching the kidney is a more serious illness, not just a worse version of the same one.\n\nFor a person with typical symptoms and no vaginal discharge or irritation, the guidance says treatment can start without a urine culture. A urine dipstick adds little in that group. It is more useful when symptoms are unclear.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover which treatments are used, how long they last, when a culture is needed, or how recurrence is managed. For those details, a clinician or pharmacist is the right person to ask.\n\nSeek urgent care now if you have fever with shaking chills, confusion, feeling very unwell, fainting, or back or side pain with fever. These can be signs of a serious reaction to infection (sepsis), and the sepsis guidance (*Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection*, Critical care and emergency medicine society guidance) says people who are unusually unwell need prompt assessment. Older adults, pregnant people, and people with diabetes, weakened immunity, or a urinary catheter are at higher risk.",
